  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nA tightening pressure gradient will remain across much of Iowa on \nSaturday as the surface cyclone over Canada deepens. As a\nresult, Saturday will be breezier than Friday. In addition, \nthis is expected to be yet another warm day, with highs once \nagain in the mid to upper 70s as stronger mixing brings down \nwarmer temperatures. At least one more day of these well-above \nnormal (near +15 degrees!) is in store on Sunday as the ridge \naxis translates eastward. \n\nBy Sunday night into Monday, a cool front associated with a surface\ncyclone over Canada will sag through the CWA. Model guidance remains\nsplit on timing for this front. The latest NBM has trended a little\nwarmer, now showing mid 60s for highs in the far north to mid 70s\nfar south. Models have also trended drier with the front keeping\nthe better rain chances across western Iowa and into portions \nof Minnesota and the Dakotas, closer to the track of a mid-level\nwave.\n\nTuesday and beyond will bring occasional chances for rain and \ncooler temperatures to the area with highs in the 60s.",
